hi this is presh talwalkar  how many squares are in this picture  give this problem a try and when you're  ready keep watching the video for the  solution  one way to solve this problem is by  counting the number of squares very  carefully  we'll remove the two squares that are in  the middle to be left with a four by  four grid  now we'll count the number of squares of  different sizes  there's exactly one square that's four  by four that's the big square and then  we'll count down the number of squares  of smaller sizes  the number of three by three squares can  be found that they're two squares per  row and we can count this for two  different rows so there's two times two  or four different squares of three by  three size  how many two by two scores are there  in a given row we can place three two by  two squares  and there are three different rows that  we can create squares so there will be  three times three which is nine two by  two squares  finally we count the number of one by  one squares  there are four different squares in a  row and we're going to have four  different rows that we can do this so  there will be four times four which  equals sixteen one by one squares  we now place the two squares in the  middle back together and we count the  number of more squares that they add  the number of big squares that they add  in the middle will be equal to two  and now they'll add some salt small  squares in the middle  each of these squares is going to  contribute four more small squares and  they're two of these so this will  contribute eight more squares in total  we add up all of these numbers and we've  counted that there are 40 squares in  this picture  did you figure it out  now there's a shortcut we could use if  we're going to do problems like this  if you have an n by n grid there will be  1 squared plus 2 squared plus all the  way up to n squared number of squares  we found this already that the number of  four by four squares was one which  equals one squared the number of three  by three squares was four which equals  two squared  the number of two by two squares  remember there were nine because there  were three squares per row and three  rows and the number of one by one  squares was 16 because there were four  per row and there were four rows  we could have started out by counting  this without even doing the count of the  squares just using this formula and then  we would have had to add on the number  of squares created by the squares in the  middle which would have been 10 and that  would have again got us to the answer  40.  so you could intuitively see why this  formula is true you count the number of  squares created per row and the number  of rows you have but let me just provide  another proof we'll use induction so  we'll assume the formula is true for n  and we'll prove it's true for n plus one  so we have an n by n grid and we're  going to add on one by one squares on  the side so that we have an n plus one  times n plus 1 grid  by the induction hypothesis the n by n  grid has a total number of hidden  squares of 1 squared plus 2 squared all  the way up to n squared number of  squares  so now we need to see the number of new  squares we can create by the scores  we've added on  we first count the number of one by one  scores we've added on  you can count this by saying there's n  plus one squared minus n squared or two  n plus one squares  another way to see this is you're adding  on  n plus one squares to the the row n plus  one to the column but the one in the  corner is going to be mutual so you have  two n plus two minus one which is again  equal to two n plus one  now you want to count the number of two  by two squares that you can create  this will be two fewer and the number of  three by three squares will also be two  fewer and this pattern continues and you  can add on exactly one square that's  size n plus one by n plus 1.  so the total number of squares we can  add on is the sum of the odd numbers up  to 2n plus 1  and that's a well-known formula of n  plus 1 squared  so we've added on a total number of n  plus 1 squared number of squares so in  total in an n plus 1 times n plus 1 grid  we have the same formula plus n plus 1  squared and that completes the induction  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel and make videos  on math and game theory you can catch me  on my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books there  are links in the video description

